#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:9
msgid "A half cell model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:20
msgid ""
"In the `previous notebook <./4-comparing-full-and-reduced-order-models."
"ipynb>`__ we saw how to compare full and reduced-order models. Both of these "
"models were posed on a single domain: the negative electrode particle. Here "
"we will see how to create a model which contains multiple domains."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:22
msgid ""
"We consider a problem posed on a half-cell geometry, which consists of a "
"separator (:math:`-L_s<x<0`) and a positive electrode (:math:`0<x<L_p`). "
"These two regions are considered \"macroscale\" domains. At each point in "
"the positive electrode we treat a \"microscale\" problem to model diffusion "
"of lithium within the positive particles (:math:`0<r<R_p`). We will see how "
"to create variables in each of the different domains so that the governing "
"equations are properly coupled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:24
msgid ""
"In the interest of simplicity we assume that the current in both the the "
"solid and electrolyte is given by Ohm's law, and ignore any concentration "
"gradients in the electrolyte. The governing equations for charge "
"conservation at the macroscale are then"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:26
msgid ""
"i_e = -\\kappa \\nabla \\phi_e, \\quad \\nabla i_e = a j, \\quad -L_s<x<0, \\"
"\\\n"
"i = -\\sigma \\nabla \\phi, \\quad \\nabla \\cdot i = -a j, \\quad 0<x<L_p,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:32
msgid ""
"where :math:`i` and :math:`i_e` are the current densities in the solid and "
"electrolyte, respectively, :math:`\\phi` and :math:`\\phi_e` are the "
"electric potentials in the solid and electrolyte, respectively, :math:"
"`\\sigma` is the solid conductivity, :math:`\\kappa` is the ionic "
"conductivity, :math:`a` is the electrode surface area per unit volume and :"
"math:`j` the interfacial current density. The charge conservation equations "
"are subject to the boundary conditions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:34
msgid ""
"\\left.\\phi_e\\right\\vert_{x=-L_s} = 0, \\quad \\left."
"i_e\\right\\vert_{x=L_p} = 0, \\quad \\left.i\\right\\vert_{x=0} = 0, \\quad "
"\\left.i\\right\\vert_{x=L_p} = \\frac{I_{\\text{app}}}{A},"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:39
msgid ""
"where :math:`I_{\\text{app}}` is the applied current and :math:`A` is the "
"electrode cross-sectional area. We then have an equation posed at each "
"macroscopic point in the electrode (:math:`0<x<L_p`) describing transport of "
"lithium within the active material particles. That is,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:41
msgid ""
"\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial t} = \\nabla \\cdot (D \\nabla c), \\quad "
"0<r<R_p,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:46
msgid "with the following boundary and initial conditions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:48
msgid ""
"\\left.\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial r}\\right\\vert_{r=0} = 0, \\quad "
"\\left.\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial r}\\right\\vert_{r=R} = -\\frac{j}{FD}, "
"\\quad \\left.c\\right\\vert_{t=0} = c_0,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:53
msgid ""
"where :math:`c` is the concentration, :math:`r` the radial coordinate, :math:"
"`t` time, :math:`R` the particle radius, :math:`D` the diffusion "
"coefficient, :math:`F` Faraday's constant, and :math:`c_0` the initial "
"concentration. For the interfacial current density we assume Butler-Volmer "
"kinetics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:55
msgid ""
"j = \\begin{cases}\n"
"        0 &-L_s<x<0 \\\\\n"
"        2 j_0(c)\\sinh\\left(\\frac{F}{2RT}(\\phi-\\phi_e-U(c))\\right) "
"&0<x<L_p\n"
"    \\end{cases},"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:63
msgid ""
"where :math:`j_0` is the exchange current density, and :math:`U` is the open-"
"circuit potential."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:75
msgid "Setting up the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:77
msgid ""
"As before, we begin by importing the PyBaMM library into this notebook, "
"along with any other packages we require, and start with an empty ``pybamm."
"BaseModel``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[19]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"%pip install \"pybamm[plot,cite]\" -q    # install PyBaMM if it is not "
"installed\n"
"import pybamm\n"
"\n"
"model = pybamm.BaseModel()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:101
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:107
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:114
msgid "Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:125
msgid ""
"Let's first define our model variables. We can define the electric potential "
"in the positive electrode in the same way as we defined the concentration "
"variables in the previous notebooks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[20]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"phi = pybamm.Variable(\"Positive electrode potential [V]\", "
"domain=\"positive electrode\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:146
msgid ""
"The potential in the electrolyte spans two domains. To set this up we first "
"define the electric potential in each of the domains"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[21]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"phi_e_s = pybamm.Variable(\"Separator electrolyte potential [V]\", "
"domain=\"separator\")\n"
"phi_e_p = pybamm.Variable(\n"
"    \"Positive electrolyte potential [V]\", domain=\"positive electrode\"\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:170
msgid ""
"and then concatenate these two variables together to define a single "
"variable that spans the separator and positive electrode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[22]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "phi_e = pybamm.concatenation(phi_e_s, phi_e_p)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:191
msgid ""
"Note that in our formulation the separator will be on the left and the "
"positive electrode will be on the right, so this is the order in which we "
"concatenated the variables for the electrolyte potential in each domain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:202
msgid ""
"The concentration in the electrode particles can vary both in :math:`r` and :"
"math:`x`, but diffusion only occurs in the :math:`r` direction. In order to "
"handle this situation we introduce the concept of \"auxiliary domains\". "
"These are domains in which quantities can vary, but spatial operators do not "
"act. To set up our concentration variable we create a ``Variable`` which has "
"domain \"positive particle\" and secondary domain \"positive electrode\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[23]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"c = pybamm.Variable(\n"
"    \"Positive particle concentration [mol.m-3]\",\n"
"    domain=\"positive particle\",\n"
"    auxiliary_domains={\n"
"        \"secondary\": \"positive electrode\",\n"
"    },\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:229
msgid ""
"Now spatial operators acting on ``c`` only act in the :math:`r` direction "
"(corresponding to the primary domain \"positive particle\"), but ``c`` can "
"still depend on :math:`x` (corresponding to the secondary domain \"positive "
"electrode\"). For more details on the different domains (primary, secondary, "
"etc.) see the `broadcasts notebook <../expression_tree/broadcasts.ipynb>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:240
msgid ""
"Next we will define our parameters. As seen before, scalar parameters can be "
"defined using the ``Parameter`` object"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[24]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"F = pybamm.Parameter(\"Faraday constant [C.mol-1]\")\n"
"R = pybamm.Parameter(\"Molar gas constant [J.mol-1.K-1]\")\n"
"T = pybamm.Parameter(\"Temperature [K]\")\n"
"\n"
"a = pybamm.Parameter(\"Surface area per unit volume [m-1]\")\n"
"R_p = pybamm.Parameter(\"Positive particle radius [m]\")\n"
"L_s = pybamm.Parameter(\"Separator thickness [m]\")\n"
"L_p = pybamm.Parameter(\"Positive electrode thickness [m]\")\n"
"A = pybamm.Parameter(\"Electrode cross-sectional area [m2]\")\n"
"\n"
"sigma = pybamm.Parameter(\"Positive electrode conductivity [S.m-1]\")\n"
"kappa = pybamm.Parameter(\"Electrolyte conductivity [S.m-1]\")\n"
"D = pybamm.Parameter(\"Diffusion coefficient [m2.s-1]\")\n"
"\n"
"I_app = pybamm.Parameter(\"Applied current [A]\")\n"
"c0 = pybamm.Parameter(\"Initial concentration [mol.m-3]\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:276
msgid ""
"Parameters can also have some functional dependence. We can define such "
"parameters using the ``FunctionParameter`` object. We also need to specify "
"the inputs (i.e. the variables on which the function depends). In our "
"example both the exchange current density and the open-circuit potential "
"will depend on the particle surface concentration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[25]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"c_surf = pybamm.surf(c)  # get the surface concentration\n"
"inputs = {\"Positive particle surface concentration [mol.m-3]\": c_surf}\n"
"j0 = pybamm.FunctionParameter(\n"
"    \"Positive electrode exchange-current density [A.m-2]\", inputs\n"
")\n"
"U = pybamm.FunctionParameter(\"Positive electrode OCP [V]\", inputs)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:302
msgid ""
"We also need to define the interfacial current, which is zero in the "
"separator and given by the Butler-Volmer equation in the positive electrode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[26]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"j_s = pybamm.PrimaryBroadcast(0, \"separator\")\n"
"j_p = 2 * j0 * pybamm.sinh((F / 2 / R / T) * (phi - phi_e_p - U))\n"
"j = pybamm.concatenation(j_s, j_p)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:325
msgid ""
"Now we can write our governing equations, boundary and initial conditions. "
"Note that we provide initial conditions for the algebraic equations. These "
"are not really initial conditions, but are used as an initial guess for the "
"solver."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[27]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# charge conservation equations\n"
"i = -sigma * pybamm.grad(phi)\n"
"i_e = -kappa * pybamm.grad(phi_e)\n"
"model.algebraic = {\n"
"    phi: pybamm.div(i) + a * j_p,\n"
"    phi_e: pybamm.div(i_e) - a * j,\n"
"}\n"
"# particle equations (mass conservation)\n"
"N = -D * pybamm.grad(c)  # flux\n"
"dcdt = -pybamm.div(N)\n"
"model.rhs = {c: dcdt}\n"
"\n"
"# boundary conditions\n"
"model.boundary_conditions = {\n"
"    phi: {\n"
"        \"left\": (pybamm.Scalar(0), \"Neumann\"),\n"
"        \"right\": (-I_app / A / sigma, \"Neumann\"),\n"
"    },\n"
"    phi_e: {\n"
"        \"left\": (pybamm.Scalar(0), \"Dirichlet\"),\n"
"        \"right\": (pybamm.Scalar(0), \"Neumann\"),\n"
"    },\n"
"    c: {\"left\": (pybamm.Scalar(0), \"Neumann\"), \"right\": (-j_p / F / D, "
"\"Neumann\")},\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"# initial conditions\n"
"inputs = {\"Initial concentration [mol.m-3]\": c0}\n"
"U_init = pybamm.FunctionParameter(\"Positive electrode OCP [V]\", inputs)\n"
"model.initial_conditions = {phi: U_init, phi_e: 0, c: c0}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:374
msgid "Finally we can add any variables of interest to the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[28]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"model.variables = {\n"
"    \"Positive electrode potential [V]\": phi,\n"
"    \"Electrolyte potential [V]\": phi_e,\n"
"    \"Positive particle concentration [mol.m-3]\": c,\n"
"    \"Positive particle surface concentration [mol.m-3]\": c_surf,\n"
"    \"Average positive particle surface concentration [mol.m-3]\": pybamm."
"x_average(\n"
"        c_surf\n"
"    ),\n"
"    \"Positive electrode interfacial current density [A.m-2]\": j_p,\n"
"    \"Positive electrode OCP [V]\": pybamm.boundary_value(U, \"right\"),\n"
"    \"Voltage [V]\": pybamm.boundary_value(phi, \"right\"),\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:407
msgid "Using the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:418
msgid ""
"As we have seen before, we can provide values for our parameters using the "
"``ParameterValues`` class. As well as providing scalar values, we also need "
"to provide the functional form using by our ``FunctionParameter`` objects. "
"Here we will define these functions locally, but we could provide the path "
"to a function defined elsewhere or provide data (see the `parameterization "
"notebook <../parameterization/parameterization.ipynb>`__)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[29]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"from pybamm import tanh\n"
"\n"
"# both functions will depend on the maximum concentration\n"
"c_max = pybamm.Parameter(\"Maximum concentration in positive electrode [mol."
"m-3]\")\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def exchange_current_density(c_surf):\n"
"    k = 6 * 10 ** (-7)  # reaction rate [(A/m2)(m3/mol)**1.5]\n"
"    c_e = 1000  # (constant) electrolyte concentration [mol.m-3]\n"
"    return k * c_e**0.5 * c_surf**0.5 * (c_max - c_surf) ** 0.5\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def open_circuit_potential(c_surf):\n"
"    stretch = 1.062\n"
"    sto = stretch * c_surf / c_max\n"
"\n"
"    u_eq = (\n"
"        2.16216\n"
"        + 0.07645 * tanh(30.834 - 54.4806 * sto)\n"
"        + 2.1581 * tanh(52.294 - 50.294 * sto)\n"
"        - 0.14169 * tanh(11.0923 - 19.8543 * sto)\n"
"        + 0.2051 * tanh(1.4684 - 5.4888 * sto)\n"
"        + 0.2531 * tanh((-sto + 0.56478) / 0.1316)\n"
"        - 0.02167 * tanh((sto - 0.525) / 0.006)\n"
"    )\n"
"    return u_eq"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:464
msgid ""
"Now we can pass these functions, along with our scalar parameters, to "
"``ParameterValues``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[30]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"param = pybamm.ParameterValues(\n"
"    {\n"
"        \"Surface area per unit volume [m-1]\": 0.15e6,\n"
"        \"Positive particle radius [m]\": 10e-6,\n"
"        \"Separator thickness [m]\": 25e-6,\n"
"        \"Positive electrode thickness [m]\": 100e-6,\n"
"        \"Electrode cross-sectional area [m2]\": 2.8e-2,\n"
"        \"Applied current [A]\": 0.9,\n"
"        \"Positive electrode conductivity [S.m-1]\": 10,\n"
"        \"Electrolyte conductivity [S.m-1]\": 1,\n"
"        \"Diffusion coefficient [m2.s-1]\": 1e-13,\n"
"        \"Faraday constant [C.mol-1]\": 96485,\n"
"        \"Initial concentration [mol.m-3]\": 25370,\n"
"        \"Molar gas constant [J.mol-1.K-1]\": 8.314,\n"
"        \"Temperature [K]\": 298.15,\n"
"        \"Maximum concentration in positive electrode [mol.m-3]\": 51217,\n"
"        \"Positive electrode exchange-current density [A.m-2]\": "
"exchange_current_density,\n"
"        \"Positive electrode OCP [V]\": open_circuit_potential,\n"
"    }\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:504
msgid ""
"Next we define the geometry. In the same way that our variable for the "
"concentration in the electrode particles had and \"auxiliary domain\", our "
"spatial variable :math:`r` also has an auxiliary domain. This means that "
"when the model in discretised there will be the correct number of particles "
"included in the geometry - one for each point in :math:`x`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[31]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"r = pybamm.SpatialVariable(\n"
"    \"r\",\n"
"    domain=[\"positive particle\"],\n"
"    auxiliary_domains={\"secondary\": \"positive electrode\"},\n"
"    coord_sys=\"spherical polar\",\n"
")\n"
"x_s = pybamm.SpatialVariable(\"x_s\", domain=[\"separator\"], "
"coord_sys=\"cartesian\")\n"
"x_p = pybamm.SpatialVariable(\n"
"    \"x_p\", domain=[\"positive electrode\"], coord_sys=\"cartesian\"\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"geometry = {\n"
"    \"separator\": {x_s: {\"min\": -L_s, \"max\": 0}},\n"
"    \"positive electrode\": {x_p: {\"min\": 0, \"max\": L_p}},\n"
"    \"positive particle\": {r: {\"min\": 0, \"max\": R_p}},\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:541
msgid ""
"Both the model and geometry can now be processed by the parameter class. "
"This replaces the parameters with the values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[32]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"param.process_model(model)\n"
"param.process_geometry(geometry)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:563
msgid ""
"We can now set up our mesh, choose a spatial method, and discretise our model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[33]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/5-half-cell-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"submesh_types = {\n"
"    \"separator\": pybamm.Uniform1DSubMesh,\n"
"    \"positive electrode\": pybamm.Uniform1DSubMesh,\n"
"    \"positive particle\": pybamm.Uniform1DSubMesh,\n"
"}\n"
"var_pts = {x_s: 10, x_p: 20, r: 30}\n"
"mesh = pybamm.Mesh(geometry, submesh_types, var_pts)\n"
"\n"
"spatial_methods = {\n"
"    \"separator\": pybamm.FiniteVolume(),\n"
"    \"positive electrode\": pybamm.FiniteVolume(),\n"
"    \"positive particle\": pybamm.FiniteVolume(),\n"
"}\n"
"disc = pybamm.Discretisation(mesh, spatial_methods)\n"
"disc.process_model(model);"
msgstr ""

